<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kursk</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kursk is a city in south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seym-river">Seym</a> river, first mentioned in 1032. It was a town of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chernigov">Chernigov</a> lands on the edge of the steppe and was destroyed in the Mongol invasion of 1240. Muscovy refounded it as a frontier fortress in 1586. In 1943 the region around it saw the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-kursk">Battle of Kursk</a>, one of the largest tank battles in history.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kursk.jpg" alt="Kursk" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Newinho · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D0%97%D0%BD%D0%B0%D0%BC%D0%B5%D0%BD%D1%81%D0%BA%D0%B8%D0%B9_%D0%A1%D0%BE%D0%B1%D0%BE%D1%80_%D0%9A%D1%83%D1%80%D1%81%D0%BA_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Kursk stands at the confluence of the Kur, Tuskar and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seym-river">Seym</a> rivers and is the administrative centre of Kursk Oblast. Its name comes from the Kur River, a name derived from kurya, meaning channel. The city was first built as a fortress on a hill dominating the plain. Its position on the shortest routes from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimea">Crimea</a> and from Moscow to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a> later made it an important trading town.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Kursk probably emerged as a fortified settlement of the Severians by the 9th century and became part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kievan-rus">Kievan Rus'</a> in the late 10th century, as a stronghold of the Kievan princes on the south-eastern border. The Life of Theodosius of the Caves mentions it in 1032, and the Tale of Igor's Campaign praises the Kursk warriors. In the early 12th century it became the seat of a separate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/appanage">appanage</a> principality. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cumans">Polovtsians</a> raided it, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/batu-khan">Batu Khan</a> devastated it; it was rebuilt, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a> had again ruined it by the end of the 13th century.</p><p>Its revival began with a new fortress built under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/feodor-i-of-russia">Feodor I of Russia</a>. Kursk was captured in 1604 by supporters of False Dmitry I and withstood Polish–Lithuanian sieges in 1612, 1616 and 1617 and another in 1634. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgorod-line">Belgorod Line</a> was built it became one of the largest trade and craft centres of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, with an annual fair held under the walls of the monastery of Our Lady of Kursk. It received city status in 1779, became the centre of Kursk Governorate in 1797 and by the mid-18th century was among the ten largest Russian cities. A fire in 1781 led to a regular plan in 1782.</p><p>German forces occupied Kursk from 4 November 1941 to 8 February 1943. In July 1943 they launched Operation Citadel, their last major offensive against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>, and the village of Prokhorovka near Kursk became the centre of one of the largest tank battles in history. Rebuilding began in February 1944, and by 1950 the urban economy was fully restored.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Kursk had 440,052 inhabitants at the 2021 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> and is the oldest city of its oblast. Its stone churches include the baroque Sergiev Cathedral and Trinity-Sergius Cathedral, both completed in 1778. The Soviet government developed it as a railway hub on the strength of nearby iron ore deposits.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kursk” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In July 1918 the Communist Party (Bolsheviks) of Ukraine was founded in Moscow, and after the Central Powers' defeat a Provisional Workers' and Peasants' Government was set up at <b>Kursk</b> in November 1918.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ukrainian-ssr">Ukrainian SSR · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Explosions in its torpedo compartment sent the nuclear submarine <b>Kursk</b> to the seabed, killing all 118 crew.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-12">12 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The Soviets lost heavily, but the German offensive at <b>Kursk</b> was halted.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-12">12 July in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The principality stretched from the Dnieper to the upper Oka and was divided into appanages such as Novgorod-Seversky and <b>Kursk</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-chernigov">Principality of Chernigov</a></li></ul></section>
